  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nIR continues to show the open wave moving northeast out of the\nCWA with the colder cloud tops into Montana and northeastern\nportions of the state. Improving skies are pushing in from the\nsouthwest that will lead to ample sun for Wednesday. WV shows\nthe main finger of the PFJ well to the north into Canada\nextending from a low over the Gulf of Alaska. Some isolated rain\nshowers persist as depicted on radar, mostly across Yellowstone\nas well as for southeastern parts of Natrona County. These will\ndissipate and push northeast by sunrise Wednesday morning with\nno impacts expected and limited accumulations. \n\nAnother weak mid level trough skirts to the north for Wednesday\nafternoon and evening that will bring some isolated storm\nchances for the northern Absarokas and Bighorns (10-20) with\nlimited hazards to have an affect. These will dissolve by sunset\nlosing its instability with radiational cooling heading into the\novernight hours Wednesday night. Diurnal winds across the wind\ncorridor will see some weak gusts up to 20 to 25 mph but most\nwill remain relatively light for Wednesday afternoon. Beyond \nthat, expect a slight warming trend for the remainder of the \nwork week with weak induced ridging building in from the south \nand southeast with increased convergence aloft extending into \nthe state. As this occurs, the aforementioned GOA low starts to \npush southeast towards the Pacific northwest becoming more of an\nopened L/W trough as it progresses towards the upper Rockies. \nCome late Friday evening and into the overnight hours, it will \nskirt to the north dropping a cold front through the CWA into \nSaturday morning. Limited moisture in the lower levels expected \nwith this more progressive system to the north, but some \ninteraction with moisture further southeast will bring sporadic \nshower activity from eastern Sweetwater to Natrona Counties \nthrough mid-day Saturday. Winds increase with this system, but \nnothing out of the ordinary for Wyoming standards with gusts up \nto 20 to 30 mph in many areas and a bit more for the wind \ncorridor in the same areas of showers. \n\nMore seasonable temperatures heading into Sunday ahead of the\nnext wave for late evening and into the overnight hours for the\nnew work week ahead. Longer term model solutions bring continued\ncooler temperatures than currently to be more seasonable for\nthis time of year heading into the later days of September. A\nmore widespread precipitation chance is possible but no \ndistinct solution is being agreed upon with the models. Stay \ntuned for updated forecasts in the coming days.",
